    historyContent: {
      origins: "Camicia is the Italian version of Beggar My Neighbour (also known as Strip Jack Naked or Beat Your Neighbor Out of Doors), a traditional children's card game played worldwide. The name 'Camicia' means 'shirt' in Italian, possibly referring to the idea of stripping your opponent of cards.",
      originCountries: [
        'Italy',
      ],
      timeline: [
        'Traditional origins: Played for generations in Italy',
        'Modern: Known as Italian variant of Beggar My Neighbour',
        "Current: Still played as children's game in Italy",
      ],
      evolution: 'The game follows the same basic structure as Beggar My Neighbour, using the Italian 40-card deck instead of the standard 52-card deck. The attack cards (A, 2, 3) and their values are consistent with the international version.',
      cultural: "Camicia is primarily a children's game in Italy, played for fun with no skill involved. It serves as an introduction to card games for young children due to its simple rules.",
    },
    setupContent: {
      players: 'Camicia is played by exactly two players. Each player receives an equal share of the deck face down (20 cards each with a 40-card Italian deck, or 26 each with 52 cards). Players keep their cards in a packet and do not look at them; they take turns turning the top card face up onto the table.',
      deck: '40-card Italian deck (suits: swords, batons, cups, coins) or 52-card standard deck. Suits are ignored. Attack cards: Aces, 2s, 3s. Normal cards: 4-10, J, Q, K (or 4-7, J, Q, K in 40-card deck).',
      equipment: '40 or 52-card deck',
      dealing: 'All cards dealt face down to the two players, 20 cards each (if 40-card deck) or 26 cards each (if 52-card deck). Players do not look at their cards.',
