        rules: {
          objective: 'Win all the cards. The player who runs out of cards loses.',
          gameplay: 'Players alternate turning the top card of their packet face up onto a central pile. Normal cards (non-attack): no action, play passes to opponent. Attack cards (A, 2, 3): opponent must play 1, 2, or 3 cards respectively. If all response cards are normal, attacker takes the entire pile and places it face down under their packet. If opponent plays an attack card during response, the original attack is cancelled and the new attack takes effect (the previous attacker must now respond). Continue until one player runs out of cards.',
          keyRules: [
            '2 players, 40 or 52 cards dealt evenly',
            'Attack cards: Aces (1 card), 2s (2 cards), 3s (3 cards)',
            'Normal cards: All others',
            'Turn cards face up to central pile alternately',
            'Attack forces opponent to play 1-3 cards',
            'If defender plays attack card, new attack supersedes old',
            'If defender plays all normal cards, attacker wins pile',
            "Won cards go face down under winner's packet",
            'First player out of cards loses',
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        strategy: {
          basic: 'There is no strategy in Camicia - it is a game of pure luck. The outcome is determined entirely by the order of cards in the initial deal.',
          intermediate: 'While no decisions are made during play, understanding the probabilities can be interesting. Attack cards (A, 2, 3) become diluted as you win cards, making you more vulnerable. Threes are most valuable but also most vulnerable to counterattack.',
          advanced: 'No advanced strategy exists as players make no choices. The game is mathematically deterministic based on initial card distribution.',
